// Generated by tools/build_site.py from content/free-questions — free practice questions.
(window.FREE_Q = window.FREE_Q || {})["rbi-b"] = {"qs": [["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question.\nDigital lending has widened access to credit for borrowers who were once ignored by formal lenders. A salaried worker in a small town can now obtain a small loan within minutes, using data from her phone instead of a long credit history. Yet speed has a cost. Some apps have concealed fees, harassed borrowers over delays and shared personal data without consent. Regulators have therefore insisted that loans be disbursed directly into the borrower's bank account and that the true annual cost be disclosed upfront. The aim is not to slow innovation but to make sure convenience does not come at the price of fairness.\nWhich of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as a problem with some lending apps?", ["Charging interest above a legal ceiling", "Concealing fees from borrowers", "Harassing borrowers over delays", "Sharing personal data without consent"], 0, "The passage lists concealed fees, harassment over delays and sharing data without consent. It mentions disclosure of the true annual cost but says nothing about any legal ceiling on interest rates."], ["English Language", "Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.\nThe new manager, along with his team, (A) / has been working hard (B) / to clear the backlog (C) / since the last two months. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 3, "'Since' is used with a point of time, while 'for' is used with a period of time. 'Two months' is a period, so the phrase should read 'for the last two months'."], ["General Awareness", "The National Bank for Agriculture and Rural Development (NABARD) was set up in 1982 on the recommendation of which committee?", ["Narasimham Committee", "M. Narasimham Working Group on Lead Banks", "B. Sivaraman Committee (CRAFICARD)", "Raghuram Rajan Committee"], 2, "NABARD was established on 12 July 1982 based on the recommendations of CRAFICARD, chaired by B. Sivaraman. The Narasimham Committees dealt with financial and banking sector reforms in the 1990s."], ["General Awareness", "Which of the following is an instrument of fiscal policy rather than monetary policy?", ["A reduction in the policy repo rate", "An increase in the Cash Reserve Ratio", "Open market sale of government securities", "A cut in personal income tax rates"], 3, "Fiscal policy is the government's use of taxation and public expenditure. Changing income tax rates is a fiscal measure, whereas repo rate, CRR and open market operations are monetary tools used by the RBI."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Pipes A and B can fill a tank in 12 hours and 15 hours respectively, while pipe C can empty the full tank in 20 hours. If all three are opened together on an empty tank, in how many hours will it be full?", ["10 hours", "8 hours", "12 hours", "9 hours"], 0, "Net filling per hour = 1/12 + 1/15 − 1/20 = (5 + 4 − 3)/60 = 1/10. So the tank fills in 10 hours. Forgetting to subtract the outlet gives a much shorter time."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Find the wrong number in the series.\n4, 6, 12, 32, 90, 315, 1260", ["32", "90", "12", "315"], 0, "Each term is multiplied by 1.5, 2, 2.5, 3, 3.5 and 4 in turn: 4, 6, 12, 30, 90, 315, 1260. The fourth term should be 12 × 2.5 = 30, so 32 is wrong."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What will come in place of the question mark (?)\n7, 8, 12, 21, 37, ?", ["61", "58", "64", "62"], 3, "The differences are 1, 4, 9 and 16, which are the squares 1², 2², 3² and 4². The next difference is 5² = 25, so the answer is 37 + 25 = 62."], ["General Awareness", "The Deposit Insurance and Credit Guarantee Corporation (DICGC), which insures bank deposits in India, is:", ["A department of the Ministry of Finance", "A subsidiary of the State Bank of India", "A wholly owned subsidiary of the Reserve Bank of India", "A public sector general insurance company"], 2, "DICGC is a wholly owned subsidiary of the RBI, set up under the DICGC Act, 1961. It insures deposits of eligible banks, including commercial banks and co-operative banks, up to a specified limit."], ["Reasoning", "Statements: All banks are lenders. Some lenders are brokers. No broker is an insurer.\nConclusions:\nI. Some banks are brokers.\nII. Some lenders are not insurers.\nWhich conclusion(s) follow?", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Either I or II follows", "Both I and II follow"], 1, "The lenders that are brokers can never be insurers, so some lenders are not insurers, and II follows. Banks need not overlap with brokers at all, so I does not follow."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "What will come in place of the question mark (?)\n25% of 640 + 3/4 of 288 − 12² = ?", ["222", "242", "232", "212"], 2, "25% of 640 = 160 and 3/4 of 288 = 216. So the value is 160 + 216 − 144 = 232. Doing percentage and fraction parts first avoids errors."], ["Reasoning", "If in a certain code CAT = 24 and DOG = 26, what is the code for BANK?", ["26", "28", "30", "32"], 1, "Each word's code is the sum of the alphabetical positions of its letters: C(3) + A(1) + T(20) = 24 and D(4) + O(15) + G(7) = 26. BANK = 2 + 1 + 14 + 11 = 28."], ["Reasoning", "Five friends A, B, C, D and E have different heights.\n• B is taller than C but shorter than A.\n• E is shorter than only one person.\n• D is taller than C.\n• A is not the tallest.\nWho is the tallest?", ["D", "E", "A", "B"], 0, "E is second tallest and A is not tallest, so the tallest is D (B and C are shorter than A). Then A is third, B fourth and C shortest: D > E > A > B > C."], ["English Language", "Choose the option that best replaces the part in capitals. If no change is needed, choose 'No improvement required'.\nIF I WOULD HAVE KNOWN about the deadline, I would have applied much earlier.", ["If I had known", "If I have known", "Had I would know", "No improvement required"], 0, "In a third conditional about an unreal past, the if-clause takes the past perfect: 'If I had known'. 'Would have' should never appear in the if-clause itself."], ["Reasoning", "Five friends A, B, C, D and E have different heights.\n• B is taller than C but shorter than A.\n• E is shorter than only one person.\n• D is taller than C.\n• A is not the tallest.\nWho is the third tallest?", ["B", "A", "E", "C"], 1, "The clues give the order D > E > A > B > C. E is second, as it is shorter than only one person, so A is third tallest."], ["Reasoning", "Riya walks 12 m towards the south, turns right and walks 5 m, then turns right again and walks 24 m. How far and in which direction is she now from her starting point?", ["13 m North-East", "17 m North-West", "12 m South-West", "13 m North-West"], 3, "Facing south, a right turn is west and the next right is north. She ends 5 m west and 24 − 12 = 12 m north of the start. Distance = √(25 + 144) = 13 m, towards the North-West."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A trader marks an item 40% above its cost price and then allows two successive discounts of 10% and 5%. What is his profit percentage?", ["25%", "21.4%", "19.7%", "20%"], 2, "Taking cost as 100, the marked price is 140. After 10% off it is 126, and after a further 5% off it is 119.7. Profit is therefore 19.7%, not simply 40 − 15 = 25%."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A boat covers 48 km downstream in 3 hours and 32 km upstream in 4 hours. What is the speed of the stream?", ["12 km/h", "4 km/h", "6 km/h", "8 km/h"], 1, "Downstream speed = 16 km/h and upstream speed = 8 km/h. Stream speed is half the difference, (16 − 8)/2 = 4 km/h. The 12 km/h figure is the boat's speed in still water."], ["Reasoning", "M is the sister of N. N is the son of P. P is the husband of Q. R is the mother of P. How is M related to R?", ["Daughter", "Niece", "Granddaughter", "Daughter-in-law"], 2, "N is P's son and M is N's sister, so M is P's daughter. R is P's mother, which makes M the daughter of R's son, that is, R's granddaughter."], ["English Language", "Choose the option that best replaces the part in capitals. If no change is needed, choose 'No improvement required'.\nAlthough she joined later, she is SENIOR THAN ME in the officers' cadre because of her rank.", ["more senior than me", "senior to me", "seniorer to me", "No improvement required"], 1, "Latin comparatives such as senior, junior, superior and inferior take 'to', not 'than'. So the correct phrase is 'senior to me'; 'more senior than' is an unnecessary double form here."], ["Reasoning", "Seven persons P, Q, R, S, T, U and V sit in a straight row facing north.\n• S sits third from the left end.\n• Only two persons sit between S and V.\n• T sits immediately to the right of V.\n• Q sits at one of the extreme ends.\n• R is an immediate neighbour of S.\n• U sits somewhere to the left of R.\nHow many persons sit between U and V?", ["Two", "Three", "Four", "One"], 1, "The final order from left is Q, U, S, R, P, V, T. U is second and V is sixth, so S, R and P, three persons, sit between them."], ["English Language", "Choose the correct meaning of the idiom: 'To take something with a pinch of salt'.", ["To regard it with some doubt", "To accept it eagerly and fully", "To add a little extra detail to it", "To deal with it very harshly"], 0, "Taking something with a pinch of salt means not believing it completely and treating it with scepticism. It has nothing to do with eager acceptance or harsh treatment."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question.\nBanks once treated climate change as a distant concern for environmental groups. That view is changing. A loan to a coastal factory may turn sour if rising seas flood its premises, and a coal plant may lose value long before its machinery wears out if cleaner energy becomes cheaper. Such risks are hard to price because they unfold over decades, while most loans are judged over a few years. Supervisors now ask banks to test how their portfolios would fare under different climate scenarios.\nAs used in the passage, the word 'sour' most nearly means:", ["taste unpleasantly acidic", "become difficult to recover", "grow larger in size", "be signed again on new terms"], 1, "In the passage a loan 'turns sour' when floods damage the borrower's premises, so the loan is unlikely to be repaid. The literal sense of an acidic taste does not fit a loan."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "The table shows loans disbursed (₹ crore) by a bank.\nYear 2021: Housing 400, Education 100\nYear 2022: Housing 450, Education 130\nYear 2023: Housing 530, Education 130\nWhat is the average annual housing loan disbursement over the three years?", ["₹450 crore", "₹475 crore", "₹445 crore", "₹460 crore"], 3, "Housing loans total 400 + 450 + 530 = 1,380 crore over three years. The average is 1,380 ÷ 3 = ₹460 crore."], ["Reasoning", "In a class of 45 students, Rohit ranks 14th from the top. Sunil is nine ranks below Rohit. What is Sunil's rank from the bottom?", ["22nd", "24th", "23rd", "21st"], 2, "Sunil is 14 + 9 = 23rd from the top. His rank from the bottom is 45 − 23 + 1 = 23rd. Forgetting to add 1 gives the tempting wrong answer 22nd."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "The present ages of A and B are in the ratio 5 : 7. After 6 years, the ratio will be 7 : 9. What is A's present age?", ["18 years", "21 years", "12 years", "15 years"], 3, "Let the ages be 5k and 7k. Then (5k + 6)/(7k + 6) = 7/9, so 45k + 54 = 49k + 42, giving k = 3. A's present age is 5 × 3 = 15 years."], ["Reasoning", "Seven persons P, Q, R, S, T, U and V sit in a straight row facing north.\n• S sits third from the left end.\n• Only two persons sit between S and V.\n• T sits immediately to the right of V.\n• Q sits at one of the extreme ends.\n• R is an immediate neighbour of S.\n• U sits somewhere to the left of R.\nWho sits exactly in the middle of the row?", ["R", "P", "S", "U"], 0, "S is third from left, so V is sixth and T seventh; Q must take the left end. R neighbours S at fourth, U fills second, P fifth: Q U S R P V T. The middle (fourth) seat is R's."], ["Reasoning", "In a certain code, CREDIT is written as XIVWRG. How is DEPOSIT written in that code?", ["WVKLHRG", "WVKLHGR", "WVLKHRG", "XVKLHRG"], 0, "Each letter is replaced by its opposite in the alphabet (A↔Z, B↔Y, C↔X and so on). Applying this, D→W, E→V, P→K, O→L, S→H, I→R, T→G gives WVKLHRG."], ["English Language", "Choose the one word for: 'A system of government in which the wealthy class rules'.", ["Oligarchy", "Plutocracy", "Autocracy", "Bureaucracy"], 1, "Plutocracy is rule by the wealthy. Oligarchy, the tempting option, means rule by a small group, which need not be defined by wealth. Autocracy is rule by one person."], ["English Language", "Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.\nNeither the chairman nor the directors (A) / was present at the meeting (B) / when the auditors (C) / presented their findings. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 1, "With 'neither ... nor', the verb agrees with the nearer subject. Here the nearer subject is the plural 'directors', so 'was present' should be 'were present'."], ["English Language", "Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.\nThe regional head insisted for (A) / an immediate review (B) / of all the pending (C) / loan applications. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 0, "The verb 'insist' takes the preposition 'on', not 'for'. The correct phrase is 'insisted on an immediate review'. The remaining parts are grammatically correct."], ["English Language", "Read the passage and answer the question.\nDigital lending has widened access to credit for borrowers who were once ignored by formal lenders. A salaried worker in a small town can now obtain a small loan within minutes, using data from her phone instead of a long credit history. Yet speed has a cost. Some apps have concealed fees, harassed borrowers over delays and shared personal data without consent. Regulators have therefore insisted that loans be disbursed directly into the borrower's bank account and that the true annual cost be disclosed upfront. The aim is not to slow innovation but to make sure convenience does not come at the price of fairness.\nWhich of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?", ["Digital lending apps should be banned because some of them harass borrowers", "Regulators want to slow down innovation in lending to protect banks", "Small-town borrowers prefer lenders who rely on long credit histories", "Digital lending widens access to credit but needs rules to keep it fair"], 3, "The passage praises wider access, lists abuses, and says rules aim to keep convenience fair without slowing innovation. It never calls for a ban, and it explicitly denies that regulators want to slow innovation."], ["English Language", "Identify the part of the sentence that contains an error.\nEach of the branch managers (A) / who attended the review meeting (B) / have been asked (C) / to submit a revised plan. (D)", ["(A)", "(B)", "(C)", "(D)"], 2, "The subject is 'Each', which is singular, so the verb must be 'has been asked'. The plural noun 'managers' inside the phrase 'of the branch managers' does not change the number of the subject."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "The average of 11 results is 50. The average of the first six results is 49 and that of the last six is 52. What is the sixth result?", ["56", "54", "58", "52"], 0, "The sixth result is counted in both groups. Sixth = (6 × 49) + (6 × 52) − (11 × 50) = 294 + 312 − 550 = 56."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "The table shows loans disbursed (₹ crore) by a bank.\nYear 2021: Housing 400, Education 100\nYear 2022: Housing 450, Education 130\nYear 2023: Housing 530, Education 130\nBy what percentage did the bank's total loans (housing + education) increase from 2021 to 2023?", ["30%", "28%", "32%", "35%"], 2, "Total loans were 400 + 100 = 500 in 2021 and 530 + 130 = 660 in 2023. The increase is 160, which is 160/500 × 100 = 32%."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Two equations I and II are given. Solve both and choose the correct relationship between x and y.\nI. x² − 11x + 30 = 0\nII. y² − 13y + 42 = 0", ["x > y", "x ≥ y", "x < y", "x ≤ y"], 3, "I factorises as (x − 5)(x − 6) = 0, so x = 5 or 6. II gives (y − 6)(y − 7) = 0, so y = 6 or 7. Since x = 6 = y is possible, the relation is x ≤ y."], ["Reasoning", "Statements: P > Q ≥ R = S, S < T ≤ U\nConclusions:\nI. P > S\nII. U > R\nWhich conclusion(s) are definitely true?", ["Only I is true", "Only II is true", "Either I or II is true", "Both I and II are true"], 3, "From P > Q ≥ R = S we get P > S, so I is true. From U ≥ T > S = R we get U > R, so II is also true. Both conclusions hold."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "Two fair dice are thrown together. What is the probability that the sum of the numbers shown is a prime number?", ["1/3", "5/12", "7/18", "4/9"], 1, "Prime sums are 2, 3, 5, 7 and 11, occurring in 1, 2, 4, 6 and 2 ways respectively, a total of 15 out of 36 outcomes. So the probability is 15/36 = 5/12."], ["Reasoning", "Study the arrangement:\nR 4 M # 7 K 2 @ T 9 P % 3 B & 6 H\nHow many symbols in the arrangement are immediately followed by a number?", ["Two", "Four", "One", "Three"], 3, "The symbols are #, @, % and &. # is followed by 7, % by 3 and & by 6, while @ is followed by the letter T. So three symbols qualify."], ["Reasoning", "How many pairs of letters are there in the word PRODUCE, each of which has as many letters between them in the word as in the English alphabet (in either direction)?", ["Two", "Three", "One", "None"], 2, "Only R–U qualifies: two letters (O, D) lie between them in the word and two (S, T) in the alphabet. Pairs such as D–C or D–E are alphabet neighbours but not word neighbours, so they fail."], ["Reasoning", "Statements: Some coins are notes. All notes are cheques. Some cheques are drafts.\nConclusions:\nI. Some coins are cheques.\nII. Some drafts are coins.\nWhich conclusion(s) follow?", ["Only conclusion I follows", "Only conclusion II follows", "Either I or II follows", "Neither I nor II follows"], 0, "Coins that are notes are also cheques, so some coins are cheques and I follows. Drafts are linked only to some cheques, which may not be the coin-notes, so II is not certain."], ["English Language", "Which of the following words is closest in meaning to PRUDENT, as in a prudent lender?", ["Reckless", "Generous", "Judicious", "Hesitant"], 2, "Prudent means acting with care and good judgement, especially about the future, which matches 'judicious'. 'Reckless' is its opposite, and 'hesitant' implies indecision rather than sound judgement."], ["Reasoning", "What is the angle between the hour hand and the minute hand of a clock at 3:40?", ["140°", "120°", "150°", "130°"], 3, "Angle = |30H − 5.5M| = |30 × 3 − 5.5 × 40| = |90 − 220| = 130°. The minute hand moves 6° per minute and the hour hand 0.5° per minute."], ["General Awareness", "Which Article of the Constitution of India provides for the constitution of a Finance Commission?", ["Article 280", "Article 112", "Article 266", "Article 148"], 0, "Article 280 requires the President to constitute a Finance Commission every five years or earlier, mainly to recommend the sharing of taxes between the Union and the States. Article 148 deals with the CAG."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "The difference between compound interest (compounded annually) and simple interest on a certain sum for 2 years at 8% per annum is ₹128. What is the sum?", ["₹16,000", "₹20,000", "₹25,000", "₹18,000"], 1, "For 2 years, CI − SI = P × (r/100)². So 128 = P × 0.0064, giving P = ₹20,000. This shortcut works only for two-year periods with annual compounding."], ["General Awareness", "The Union Budget is presented to Parliament as the 'Annual Financial Statement' under which Article of the Constitution?", ["Article 110", "Article 112", "Article 280", "Article 265"], 1, "Article 112 requires a statement of estimated receipts and expenditure of the Government of India for each financial year, called the Annual Financial Statement. Article 110 defines a Money Bill."], ["General Awareness", "The Liquidity Coverage Ratio under Basel III requires banks to hold high-quality liquid assets sufficient to cover total net cash outflows over which period of stress?", ["7 days", "90 days", "30 days", "One year"], 2, "LCR measures short-term resilience: the stock of high-quality liquid assets should cover net cash outflows over a 30-day stress period. The one-year horizon relates to the Net Stable Funding Ratio instead."], ["General Awareness", "When the RBI began operations in April 1935, where was its Central Office located before it moved permanently to Mumbai in 1937?", ["New Delhi", "Calcutta (Kolkata)", "Madras (Chennai)", "Nagpur"], 1, "The RBI's Central Office was first set up in Calcutta and shifted permanently to Bombay (now Mumbai) in 1937, where it remains, housing the Governor's office and policy departments."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A train 240 m long crosses a platform 360 m long in 30 seconds. What is the speed of the train?", ["64 km/h", "80 km/h", "72 km/h", "54 km/h"], 2, "The train must cover its own length plus the platform: 600 m in 30 s, which is 20 m/s. Converting, 20 × 18/5 = 72 km/h."], ["General Awareness", "The Statutory Liquidity Ratio (SLR) that banks must maintain is prescribed under which law?", ["Section 42 of the RBI Act, 1934", "Section 17 of the RBI Act, 1934", "Section 24 of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949", "Section 35A of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949"], 2, "SLR is mandated by Section 24 of the Banking Regulation Act, 1949. Section 42 of the RBI Act, 1934 is the tempting distractor because it governs the Cash Reserve Ratio, not SLR."], ["English Language", "Choose the word that best fills the blank.\nThe central bank's decision to leave rates unchanged was widely ________ by economists, most of whom had predicted a pause.", ["deterred", "exempted", "anticipated", "retracted"], 2, "The economists had predicted a pause, so the decision was expected, that is, 'anticipated'. 'Deterred', 'exempted' and 'retracted' do not make sense with a decision that economists had forecast."], ["Quantitative Aptitude", "A starts a business with ₹40,000. Five months later B joins with ₹60,000. At the end of the year the total profit is ₹45,000. What is B's share?", ["₹24,000", "₹22,500", "₹27,000", "₹21,000"], 3, "Capital-time products are A = 40,000 × 12 = 4,80,000 and B = 60,000 × 7 = 4,20,000, a ratio of 8 : 7. B's share = 45,000 × 7/15 = ₹21,000."], ["General Awareness", "If the Indian rupee depreciates sharply against the US dollar, other things remaining equal, what is the most likely immediate effect?", ["Imported crude oil becomes cheaper in rupees", "India's external debt falls in rupee terms", "Foreign travel becomes cheaper for Indians", "Indian exports become cheaper for buyers abroad"], 3, "A weaker rupee means a foreign buyer needs fewer dollars to buy the same Indian goods, so exports become more competitive. Imports, foreign travel and dollar-denominated debt all become costlier in rupee terms."], ["English Language", "Choose the word most nearly OPPOSITE in meaning to AUSTERITY.", ["Severity", "Thrift", "Discipline", "Extravagance"], 3, "Austerity means strict economy and the avoidance of luxury. Its opposite is extravagance, meaning lavish spending. 'Thrift' and 'discipline' are close in meaning to austerity, not opposite."], ["General Awareness", "Which liquidity tool lets the RBI absorb surplus funds from banks without having to give them government securities as collateral in return?", ["Standing Deposit Facility", "Marginal Standing Facility", "Fixed-rate reverse repo", "Term repo auction"], 0, "The Standing Deposit Facility absorbs liquidity without collateral, removing the constraint of the RBI's securities holdings. Reverse repo needs collateral, while MSF and term repo inject liquidity rather than absorb it."], ["English Language", "Choose the pair of words that best fills the blanks.\nEver since the company ________ its distribution network, its rural sales ________ steadily.", ["has expanded / grew", "expands / had grown", "had expanded / will grow", "expanded / have grown"], 3, "'Ever since' with a past event (simple past 'expanded') is followed by the present perfect for a continuing result, 'have grown'. The other pairs mix tenses that do not fit this structure."], ["General Awareness", "Under Basel III norms, the Capital Conservation Buffer must be held in the form of:", ["Additional Tier 1 perpetual bonds", "Common Equity Tier 1 capital", "Tier 2 subordinated debt", "General provisions and loss reserves"], 1, "The Capital Conservation Buffer is meant to absorb losses in stress without breaching minimum requirements, so it must consist of the highest-quality capital, Common Equity Tier 1. Bonds and provisions do not qualify."], ["General Awareness", "A bank is checking which of its holdings count towards its SLR requirement. Which of the following would NOT qualify?", ["Rated bonds issued by a private manufacturing company", "Cash held in its own vaults", "Unencumbered Central Government securities", "Gold valued as per RBI norms"], 0, "SLR can be held in cash, gold and unencumbered approved securities such as Government securities. Corporate bonds, however safe or well rated, are not SLR-eligible assets."], ["General Awareness", "A Gini coefficient of zero for a country would indicate:", ["Perfect inequality, with one person earning all income", "Perfect equality of income distribution", "Zero growth in national income", "An equal number of rich and poor households"], 1, "The Gini coefficient ranges from 0 to 1 (or 0 to 100). A value of 0 means everyone has the same income, while a value of 1 means a single person receives all the income."], ["General Awareness", "In which year was the Reserve Bank of India nationalised, ending its status as a shareholders' bank?", ["1935", "1969", "1949", "1955"], 2, "The RBI began operations in 1935 as a privately held shareholders' bank and was nationalised with effect from 1 January 1949. The year 1969 is associated with the nationalisation of 14 major commercial banks, not the RBI."], ["General Awareness", "In India's balance of payments, a Foreign Direct Investment inflow is recorded in which account?", ["Capital (financial) account", "Current account", "Merchandise trade account", "Invisibles account"], 0, "FDI creates ownership claims on assets, so it is recorded in the capital (financial) account. The current account covers trade in goods and services, primary income and transfers; invisibles are a part of it."]]};
